+# Evidence Contract: Pulseboard
+**Project**: Pulseboard
+**Project ID**: `001-pulseboard`
+**Project Archetype**: b2b_saas
+**Play Level**: build
+**Speck Version**: 10.5.0
+**Last Updated**: 2026-08-10
+## 0. How this contract is read — the Four Principles
+This is the proof contract, not a product specification. A gate seeks a defect first; every claim needs an observed runtime mechanism; an unreachable flow is a finding; and an independent evaluator must try to break the evidence. A capture without a recorded judgment is surrogate proof.
+## 1. Target Launch Platforms
+No recipe, runtime, or distribution configuration exists in this workspace. The reversible working assumption is a browser-delivered B2B SaaS; changing the target requires updating this contract before a readiness claim.
+| Platform | Build artifact | Distribution |
+|----------|----------------|--------------|
+| Web | Production-optimized browser bundle containing Pulseboard | Configured production web host; not yet evidenced |
+## 2. Valid Proof Sources (per platform)
+### Platform: Web
+- A clean production build, served as the built artifact rather than a development server, with its commit SHA recorded.
+- Browser-driven recordings, screenshots, accessibility trees, console/network logs, and measured interaction results from that build.
+- For authorization: a live read or attempted write as each real least-privileged workspace principal, in both permitted and forbidden directions.
+- For reminder delivery: a pre-action baseline plus a post-action read-back of the delivery record and delivery-provider outcome for the same reminder id.
+- For confirmation: a recording and backend trace showing the exact action text, owner, and due date presented before the user confirms.
+- A separately authored adversarial audit and LARP judgment attached to the evidence, not an implementer assertion.
+### Claim-type admissibility
+| Claim type | Admissible evidence | Does not count |
+|------------|---------------------|----------------|
+| `correctness` | tests, built-runtime traces, static checks | none categorically; the claim must name its mechanism |
+| `visibility` | live-probe or database read as real allowed and denied workspace principals | mocked or bypass-role reads, endpoint 404 alone, screenshots |
+| `acceptance` | request executed against the deployed target or real runtime boundary | mocked clients or hand-written fixtures without provenance |
+| `persistence` | pre-action baseline and post-action datastore read-back | success screenshot or post-action-only read |
+| `fit` | platform accessibility tree plus measured geometry at three widths | jsdom geometry, props assertions, unmeasured capture |
+### Pulseboard promise discharge table
+| Promise | Falsifiable runtime predicate | Required discharge artifact |
+|---------|-------------------------------|----------------------------|
+| PRM-001 | A reminder cannot enter the scheduled state until the user has explicitly confirmed the displayed action text, owner, and due date. | `live-probe:specs/projects/001-pulseboard/larp-recordings/<sha>-confirmation.log` plus capture of the confirmation step |
+| PRM-002 | A principal from workspace B cannot read workspace A's transcript, action, or delivery status, while workspace A can read its own record. | `live-probe:specs/projects/001-pulseboard/larp-recordings/<sha>-workspace-isolation.log` |
+| PRM-003 | For one confirmed reminder, the system records exactly one delivery attempt and either one delivery outcome or a truthful terminal failure state visible to the user. | `db-catalog:specs/projects/001-pulseboard/larp-recordings/<sha>-delivery-readback.log` plus `capture:` of the terminal user state |
